# 🐞 Troubleshooting Notes

This file documents technical issues encountered while building the Active Directory cybersecurity lab, along with the steps taken to resolve them.

---

## 🧩 1. Windows Client Could Not Join the Domain

**Error:**
> "The specified domain either does not exist or could not be contacted."

**Cause:**
- Windows 10 client couldn't resolve `lab.local` due to DNS misconfiguration

**Fix:**
- Changed DNS settings on the client to point to the domain controller (192.168.56.10)
- Verified connectivity with `ping` and `nslookup`
- Successfully joined the domain after correction

---

## 🔐 2. User Couldn't Log In (Incorrect Username or Password)

**Error:**
> "The username or password is incorrect."

**Cause:**
- Password not set for user
- Password was set to change at next login
- Typing the wrong username format (e.g., just `SarahB` instead of `SarahB@lab.local`)

**Fix:**
- Reset password in Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC)
- Removed "User must change password at next login" checkbox
- Ensured correct login format: `username@domain.local` (e.g., `SarahB@lab.local`)

---

## 🚫 3. “This sign-in method isn’t allowed” (Blocked by Group Policy)

**Error:**
> "The sign-in method you're trying to use isn't allowed."

**Cause:**
- Group Policy Object (GPO) was not configured to allow the user’s group to log on locally

**Fix:**
- Edited GPO under `Local Policies > User Rights Assignment`
- Added the correct group (e.g., `hr-staff`) to **Allow log on locally**
- Applied GPO with `gpupdate /force`

---

## ❌ 4. Workstation Not Showing Up in Active Directory

**Error:**
> Computer object not visible in AD

**Cause:**
- Client VM not joined to the domain
- Improper DNS configuration

**Fix:**
- Verified VM network was set to Host-Only Adapter
- Pointed client DNS to the server IP
- Successfully joined domain
- Moved the computer object to correct OU

---

## 🖧 5. Ping Fails Between Client and Server

**Error:**
> "Ping: Destination host unreachable" or "Request timed out"

**Cause:**
- Network adapters set to NAT instead of Host-Only
- Firewall may be blocking ping
- Wrong IP or subnet configuration

**Fix:**
- Switched both VMs to Host-Only Adapter
- Set static IPs and confirmed they were on the same subnet
- Disabled Windows Firewall temporarily to confirm connectivity
- Used `ping` and `gpresult` to validate communication

---

## 🔁 6. Cloned VM Didn't Appear in AD

**Issue:**
- After cloning a Windows 10 VM, it didn’t show up in the AD OU

**Cause:**
- The cloned machine retained the same SID or didn’t rejoin the domain properly

**Fix:**
- Renamed the machine
- Rejoined it to the domain using a domain admin account
- Moved computer object to correct OU (`HR-Computers`, `IT-Computers`, etc.)

---

## 🔒 7. Local Login Works, But Domain Login Fails for a User

**Error:**
> User logs in locally but can't use domain account

**Cause:**
- GPO didn't allow that group to log on locally
- The computer wasn't in the right OU to receive the GPO

**Fix:**
- Ensured the correct GPO was linked to the OU where the workstation resides
- Used `gpresult /r` on the client to verify GPO application

---

## 🧼 8. Account Locked or Disabled

**Issue:**
- Account appeared to be locked or disabled

**Cause:**
- Multiple failed login attempts
- Account settings in ADUC

**Fix:**
- Unlocked user in ADUC by right-clicking → Reset Password
- Enabled the account if it was disabled
- Tested login again successfully

---

## 📁 9. Shared Folder Not Accessible

**Error:**
> "Access denied" when trying to reach a shared folder

**Cause:**
- NTFS or Share permissions not assigned to the user's group

**Fix:**
- Right-clicked shared folder → Properties → Security
- Added `hr-staff` with read/write permissions
- Ensured folder was properly shared and accessible

---

## 🎨 10. GPO Background Not Applying

**Issue:**
- Desktop wallpaper did not change despite GPO settings

**Cause:**
- Network location (UNC path) was inaccessible at logon
- GPO not enforced or not linked to the right OU

**Fix:**
- Used `gpupdate /force` and `gpresult /r` to check policy application
- Placed wallpaper file on a local path or accessible shared folder
- Ensured loopback policy processing was enabled (if needed)

